---
.title = "A Rambling Review of Hi-Fi RUSH",
.date = @date("2026-04-25T16:47:04+0000"),
.author = "Garrett Moseke",
.layout = "post.shtml",
.draft = false,
.description = "Guy who only uses his guitar to hit robots is still better at guitar than me :(",
.tags = ["Rambling Review"," Video Games"],
.aliases = [],
---

Hi-Fi RUSH (yes the game has the RUSH bit in all caps, and I'll be damned if I'm not gonna
type it out every time) is on oddball of a game. The premise is pretty excellent: it's Devil May Cry
but as a rhythm game. And to the developer's credit, they take this to the absolute
extreme: *everything* is moving to the beat. Enemies attack to the beat. Pipes in the
level expand and contract to the beat. The UI itself is jamming along. Hell even Chai's
gait goes to the beat, which is a really great little touch that I'm sure 99% of people
didn't even notice.

And the beats are good! I would be pretty dissapointed if a game that revolved around its
soundtrack had a bad one, but thankfully that's not the case here. It's not an all timer
of artistic creation by any means, but it is *fun*, and that's all that really matters
when the whole point is to tap your foot to it. It leans hard into the pop-rock aesthetic,
and they embrace that with the visuals too, with a heavy comic-book style and 90s-esque
robot enemies.

It's good that Hi-Fi RUSH has such a great style, because sadly for me the gameplay itself
just isn't quite there. Combat is fun for a while, but by the end it felt mostly like a
spamfest, especially with how low the cooldowns on your companions are compared to how
strong they are (I spent most of combat mashing both triggers to cycle through them as
fast as possible, and at one point completely stunlocked a boss without even having to
bother attacking to the beat). There's a parry system that feels a bit tacked-on, so much
so that some enemies pull you out of combat to force you into a parry mini-game in order
to defeat them.

There's lots more of "forced rhythm" things like that in Hi-Fi RUSH than I'd like. I
wouldn't mind it so much if the fail states weren't so frustrating: almost every time it
required me re-prompting to begin again and having to watch a second or two mini animation
or cutscene. This doesn't sound like much, but for particularly complex sequences it gets
annoying very fast the more you mess up. I wish that the game would have trusted itself
more and played up the "action to the rhythm" core mechanic more, but without forcing it
to be a dedicated thing in and of itself. They even already had this in a few places: jump pads and
buttons that you had to use or hit on-beat, but without guitar-hero style button prompts flying
across the screen.

This extends to the platforming as well. Something about it just felt a bit off to me, the
best explanation I can give is that Chai's dash and air movement just didn't quite feel
fast or "crisp" enough. I understand that they have to break up the gameplay somehow so
that there's variety, but I do think that the platforming could have used a bit more of a
speedup. The best parts for me were always when it was leaning into the fast-paced
rock-and-roll style with grappling sequences and rail grinds, rather than when I had to
hop between a few floating platforms for the umpteenth time.

I realize I've been mostly negative here, but I do want to say that I enjoyed my time with
Hi-Fi RUSH. On the whole it's a fun experience, and it definitely doesn't overstay
its welcome - I finished the whole thing in just under 9 hours and that felt just about
right to me. I think I'd label it as a poster child of style over substance, but in a good
way. The soundtrack bops, and the rest of the game is competent enough that that's plenty
for me to have a good time. I recommend it, but I would personally only pick it up on sale.
